WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:09.885
Two servers both think they are the leader and both write to storage, corrupting it. Leases and fencing tokens stop the old leader.

00:00:11.085 --> 00:00:12.014
Pause and think.

00:00:12.414 --> 00:00:14.945
What does a fencing token protect against?

00:00:15.345 --> 00:00:22.659
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:31.255
Many systems need exactly one node in charge. Leader election picks it, and leases make sure an old leader steps down.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:41.545
New word: leader election. Machines choosing one coordinator, and a new one if it fails. Picture it like this: Choosing a new team captain when the old one leaves.

00:00:41.945 --> 00:00:51.593
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:00:51.993 --> 00:01:00.167
New word: lease. Permission that automatically expires unless renewed. Picture it like this: A library loan with a due date.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:06.877
Think of a group project where one person holds the only marker for the whiteboard.

00:01:07.277 --> 00:01:10.679
Whoever holds the marker writes; everyone else reads.

00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:16.617
If that person leaves the room for too long, the group votes and gives a new marker to someone else.

00:01:17.017 --> 00:01:22.207
The old person must know their marker has expired, or two people would write over each other.

00:01:23.407 --> 00:01:26.925
Here is a Raft style election. The leader sends heartbeats.

00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:37.350
New word: Raft. A consensus algorithm with an elected leader replicating a log to followers. Picture it like this: A team captain who keeps everyone's notebook in sync.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.772
Followers reset their timers.

00:01:41.172 --> 00:01:43.460
The leader crashes, and heartbeats stop.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.747
One follower's random timer fires first. It becomes a candidate for term two.

00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:50.436
It asks for votes,

00:01:50.836 --> 00:01:52.984
and wins with a majority of three.

00:01:54.184 --> 00:01:56.750
Avoiding two leaders, called split brain.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.459
Require a majority, so two groups can never both win.

00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:06.257
Use a term or epoch number, a fencing token, and reject writes from older terms.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:18.227
New word: fencing token. An ever-increasing number given to each new leader so storage rejects writes from old leaders. Picture it like this: A newer ticket number that makes old tickets invalid.

00:02:19.827 --> 00:02:25.179
Leases give the leader a time limited right to act; it must stop before the lease ends.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:34.239
Majority votes pick one leader, terms and fencing tokens reject old leaders, and leases bound how long anyone can act alone.

00:02:35.477 --> 00:02:36.731
Back to the question.

00:02:37.131 --> 00:02:39.662
What does a fencing token protect against?

00:02:40.062 --> 00:02:43.916
A paused old leader waking up and writing after a new leader was chosen.

00:02:45.116 --> 00:02:45.987
Quick recap.

00:02:46.387 --> 00:02:48.535
Exactly one leader at a time.

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:50.096
Leases expire.

00:02:50.496 --> 00:02:52.888
Fencing tokens reject stale leaders.
